===========================================================================
 THE CULT  --  Project 2/3 (hardened + isolated)
 A hidden-role social-deduction game over UNIX stream sockets, with a
 cgroups-v2 + seccomp isolation sandbox for players.
 Author: Abhishek Singh
 Course: CSE/CEN Advanced Operating Systems, Arizona State University, Fall 2026
===========================================================================

WHAT THIS IS
------------
The Cult is a text-based, Werewolves-style game for a Linux machine. Each
player is a separate Linux user running a client process; a single server
process (the "Prophet") runs the game. A hidden Cult Leader CONVERTS players
one at a time; converted players secretly join a growing multicast "cult
channel" while keeping up appearances in the public day chat. Nobody is ever
eliminated, so a connection stays open for the whole game.

Project 1 used UNIX domain stream sockets multiplexed with a selectors/epoll
event loop. For Projects 2/3 the game is improved in two ways that answer the
security and robustness feedback classmates gave on Project 1:

  (A) The communications library was HARDENED (see cultnet.py): the event
      loop is now fully non-blocking, oversized/slow frames can no longer
      hang the server, and the kernel-verified UID is used as an access
      control gate (allowlist + one-connection-per-UID + 0660 socket).

  (B) A NEW ISOLATION MECHANISM was added (see cultsandbox.py): each player
      runs inside a cgroups-v2 control group (CPU/memory/pids caps) and under
      a seccomp-BPF syscall filter, so one malicious player cannot crash the
      server, starve the machine, or escape to run arbitrary programs.

THE COMMUNICATIONS LIBRARY (single file):   cultnet.py


FILES
-----
  cultnet.py       THE communications library (single file). UNIX stream
                   sockets + selectors/epoll + length-prefixed JSON framing +
                   SO_PEERCRED. Project 2/3 adds the non-blocking reader,
                   frame-size cap, slowloris timeout, UID allowlist,
                   one-per-UID, and 0660 socket mode.
  cultsandbox.py   THE NEW ISOLATION MECHANISM. A launcher that wraps a
                   player in a cgroup v2 (cpu.max/memory.max/pids.max) and a
                   raw seccomp-BPF syscall filter, then runs the client
                   in-process. Also hosts built-in attack "victims" for
                   testing (--victim cpuhog|memhog|forkbomb|execshell|ptrace).
  cultserver.py    The game server / moderator. Unchanged game logic; now
                   passes the allowlist / one-per-UID / socket mode to the
                   library from the config.
  cultclient.py    The player client (unchanged).
  cultbot.py       Automated bot player, for the headless demo.
  cultconfig       Server config (timers, roles, and the P2/P3 hardening
                   flags enforceUids / onePerUid / socketMode).
  democonfig       Config for the single-user bot demo (hardening off).
  install.sh       Full install: cultmaster + player users, the 'cult' group,
                   the UID allowlist, and the delegated cgroup parent.
  setup_cgroups.sh Prepares /sys/fs/cgroup/cult (run as root; re-run after a
                   reboot since cgroup settings are not persistent).
  mkplayer.sh      Creates one playerN user, adds it to 'cult', installs the
                   client + library + sandbox.
  makeplayers.py   Creates player0..playerN-1 (calls mkplayer.sh).
  uninstall.sh     Removes the users this created.
  measure.py       Runs the attacks with/without caps and writes CSVs
                   (cpu.csv, mem.csv, pids.csv) proving the cgroup limits.
  plot.py          Renders the result figures (fig_dos/cpu/mem/pids .png).
  README           This file.


COMMUNICATIONS LIBRARY API  (cultnet.py)
----------------------------------------
Primitive: UNIX domain STREAM sockets (AF_UNIX, SOCK_STREAM) multiplexed in
one server process with a non-blocking `selectors` (epoll) event loop.
Messages are length-prefixed JSON frames: a 4-byte big-endian length followed
by that many bytes of UTF-8 JSON. Peer identity comes from SO_PEERCRED.

SERVER (class CultServer):
    CultServer(sock_path, allowed_uids=None, one_per_uid=False,
               sock_mode=0o660, max_frame=65536, out_max=1<<20,
               partial_timeout=10.0)
    .poll(timeout) -> [events]   pump the loop once; events are
                                   ('connect', cid, uid)
                                   ('message', cid, obj)
                                   ('disconnect', cid)
    .send(cid, obj)              UNICAST one JSON object to one connection
    .multicast(cids, obj)        MULTICAST to a chosen subset
    .broadcast(obj)              BROADCAST to every connection
    .peers() -> [cid]            connected connection ids
    .uid_of(cid) -> uid          kernel-verified UID of a connection
    .close()                     shut down and remove the socket

CLIENT (class CultClient):
    CultClient(sock_path); .send(obj); .recv(timeout=None) -> obj; .close()


SETUP (Ubuntu 26.04 VM, cgroup v2, with sudo)
---------------------------------------------
From inside the unpacked source directory:

    sudo ./install.sh 6

This creates cultmaster, players player0..player5 (each in the 'cult'
group), the UID allowlist, and the delegated cgroup parent. If the host is
not cgroup v2 the sandbox still runs seccomp-only and says so.

After a reboot, re-prepare the cgroup parent:   sudo ./setup_cgroups.sh


PLAYING (under the isolation sandbox)
-------------------------------------
1. Start the server as cultmaster:
       sudo -u cultmaster bash -c 'cd /home/cultmaster && python3 cultserver.py'

2. For each player, in its own terminal, join THROUGH the sandbox:
       sudo -u player0 python3 /home/player0/cultsandbox.py \
            --sock /home/cultmaster/cult.sock --name player0
   (defaults: cpu 20%, mem 128 MB, pids 64; override with --cpu/--mem/--pids)

Type to chat; actions are  pitch/check/vote <name>.


QUICK LOCAL DEMO (one user, no install)
---------------------------------------
    python3 cultserver.py democonfig &
    ./rundemo.sh 5          # 5 bots play a full game on a local /tmp socket


TESTING THE ISOLATION (produces the figures)
--------------------------------------------
    sudo python3 setup_cgroups.sh         # once, on the cgroup-v2 VM
    sudo python3 measure.py all           # writes cpu.csv, mem.csv, pids.csv
    python3 plot.py . .                   # renders fig_cpu/mem/pids .png

Seccomp can be tested with no cgroups at all:
    python3 cultsandbox.py --no-cgroup --victim execshell   # killed (SIGSYS)
    python3 cultsandbox.py --no-cgroup --victim ptrace      # killed (SIGSYS)


WHY THIS IS SAFER THAN PROJECT 1
--------------------------------
  * Non-blocking event loop: one slow/oversized/slowloris client can no
    longer freeze the single server thread (the Project 1 DoS).
  * SO_PEERCRED is now an access gate: only allowlisted player UIDs connect,
    at most one connection per UID, over a 0660 (not 0777) socket.
  * cgroups v2: a busy/fork-bombing/memory-bombing player is capped inside
    its own control group and cannot starve the server or the machine.
  * seccomp-BPF: a compromised client cannot exec a shell, ptrace, load
    kernel modules, mount filesystems, etc. -- it is confined to its socket.


UNINSTALL
---------
    sudo ./uninstall.sh 6
